analysis:
Examined the base text of provider-swift/Tests/ProviderCoreTests/Inference/Vision/QwenVLMTargetExtractionTests.swift (base lines 1-987) and the head patch excerpt, which covers head lines 335-366. The change is test-only. It adds endOfPromptBoundaryRequiresDensePagedSSD, which exercises EngineV2Factory.demandedLatestCheckpointMinimumTokens and demandedCaptureBoundaryMinimumTokens. The new test mirrors the existing shortDemandedCaptureRequiresDensePagedSSD test (base lines 316-335). It asserts that end-of-prompt checkpoint demand applies only to a dense Qwen model with a paged backend and an actual SSD store, and that it keeps store.config.minEffectiveTokens as the floor. It asserts nil for MoE, contiguous-backend and nil-store cases, and that the capture-boundary partition stays GLM-only. A second fixture checks a non-paged (contiguous) store; that part of the excerpt is truncated.

Relevant threat: T-041, covering the prefix-cache at-rest surface and per-donation gating. New end-of-prompt checkpoint donations may widen which prefixes persist to the encrypted SSD tier. The production implementation is not in this batch: EngineV2Factory demandedLatestCheckpointMinimumTokens, the checkpoint store, and the donation gating in SSDPrefixCache.donate. Integration should verify the following in the production files:
- the minEffectiveTokens floor is preserved;
- cacheSalt/scope folding into HMAC tags remains intact;
- the DARKBLOOM_PREFIX_CACHE kill switch is still honored;
- the per-donation adoption-bound/benefit gating is not loosened.

The tests themselves introduce no attack surface, secrets or security-control changes. No regression was identified from the supplied evidence.
